# Cloudflare Pages `_headers`. Copied verbatim into `dist/` by the build, so
# these rules apply to every response the site serves.
#
# The one rule that matters most is the HTML cache. Pages serves `index.html`
# for every client-side route via `_redirects`, so if that file is cached even
# briefly, a deploy ships stale asset filenames to every visitor and those
# fingerprints 404. Fingerprinted assets go the other way and are cached
# forever, because their contents can never change under the same name.
#
# Not here on purpose: a Content-Security-Policy. This page loads the Meta Pixel
# and Google Analytics, both of which inject inline scripts. A policy written
# without watching those requests fail in production is worse than no policy —
# the breakage is silent and only visible in a browser console nobody opens.
# Add one once the tag set is settled, with `report-only` first.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Vite content-hashes these filenames, so a given name always has the same
# bytes and can be cached permanently.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The brand images are referenced by name from index.html and from the content
# files rather than fingerprinted, so they get a shorter leash than /assets.
/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
